# Plain-language replies (layman's terms)

## Default (every final reply)

End with a **short summary** (3–6 sentences max) that a non-technical reader can follow:

- Use everyday words. Avoid jargon (API, middleware, DSN, CORS, etc.) unless the user already used them.
- Say **what changed** and **why it matters** to using the product — not file names, line numbers, or stack traces unless debugging.
- Prefer outcomes: “errors get reported automatically” not “Sentry init in sentry-init.js”.
- Keep the technical body of the reply as needed for implementation; the **closing summary is always layman-friendly**.

## When the user asks to explain

Only then expand in detail — still in **plain language**:

- Use analogies and step-by-step “what you would click / see”.
- Still avoid unnecessary jargon; if a technical term is required, define it in one short phrase.
- Do not dump long code blocks unless they asked for code or a fix.

## Examples

**Good closing summary:**  
“You can open the admin area after logging in. We also hooked up error reporting so when something breaks in the app, you get an email-style alert in Sentry instead of only finding out from users.”

**Avoid as the only summary:**  
“Implemented `ProtectedRoute` state passthrough and optional `VITE_SENTRY_DSN` with dynamic import in `main.jsx`.”
